# Coding Standards (Main Reference)

**Primary reference project:** `D:\work\MicroServices\Ecommerce\Ecommerce`

When implementing features, mirror the **modern Catalog / Basket / Discount** style in that repo (not Ordering — it is older and inconsistent). Prefer looking at those services for layering, CQRS, validation, Result, mapping, and API patterns before inventing new ones.

## Methods

- Keep methods small and focused on a single responsibility.
- Extract private methods when a method becomes large or difficult to read.

## Architecture (Clean Architecture + CQRS)

Per feature/service use four layers:

| Layer | Project | Contains |
|-------|---------|----------|
| Api | `*.Api` | Controllers, `Program.cs`, DI, `ResultExtensions` |
| Application | `*.Application` | Commands, Queries, Handlers, Validators, Behaviors, Mappers, Responses, Constants |
| Core | `*.Core` | Entities, repository interfaces, specs/pagination |
| Infrastructure | `*.Infrastructure` | Repository implementations, DbContext, persistence |

Dependency direction: **Api → Application → Core ← Infrastructure**.

Application folder layout:

```text
Application/
  Behaviors/
  Commands/
  Constants/
  Handlers/Commands/
  Handlers/Queries/
  Mappers/
  Queries/
  Responses/
  Validators/
```

## CQRS (MediatR)

- Commands/Queries are `sealed record` implementing `IRequest<Result>` or `IRequest<Result<T>>` (FluentResults).
- Naming: `CreateProductCommand`, `GetProductByIdQuery`.
- Handlers are `sealed class` implementing `IRequestHandler<TRequest, Result|Result<T>>`.
- Handler folders: `Handlers/Commands/`, `Handlers/Queries/`.
- Controllers stay thin: `_mediator.Send(...)` then `return result.ToHttpResponse()`.

## DTOs / Responses

- Response DTOs live in `Application/Responses/`.
- All DTOs (and Commands/Queries used as request bodies) must be `sealed record`.
- Naming: `{Entity}ResponseDto`.

## Entities

- Domain entities live in Core.
- Prefer a shared base (`BaseEntity` / `EntityBase`) when useful.
- Use static factory methods for entity creation when introducing domain logic (stricter than Ecommerce POCOs).

## Validation (FluentValidation)

- Use FluentValidation; one validator per Command/Query.
- Validators must be `sealed` and inherit `AbstractValidator<T>`.
- Do **not** validate manually inside handlers — register MediatR `ValidationBehavior` that turns failures into `Result.Fail` / `Result.Fail<T>`.
- Put length/rule limits in static `*Constants` / `ValidationConstants`.

```csharp
public sealed class CreateProductCommandValidator
    : AbstractValidator<CreateProductCommand>
{
    public CreateProductCommandValidator()
    {
        RuleFor(x => x.Name)
            .NotEmpty()
            .MaximumLength(ValidationConstants.NameMaxLength);
    }
}
```

Pipeline behaviors (Application): `UnhandledExceptionBehavior`, `LoggingBehavior`, `ValidationBehavior`.

## Result Pattern (FluentResults)

- Handlers return `FluentResults.Result` or `Result<T>` — **not** a custom `ResultDto<T>`.
- Success: `Result.Ok(value)` / `Result.Ok()`.
- Failure: `Result.Fail("…")` / `Result.Fail<T>("…")`.
- API maps via `ToHttpResponse()` (success → 200, failure → 400 with errors).

```csharp
Task<Result<ProductResponseDto>> Handle(
    CreateProductCommand request,
    CancellationToken cancellationToken);
```

## Mapping

- Use **Mapperly** (`Riok.Mapperly`) for all object mapping.
- `[Mapper] public partial class {Entity}Mapper` with methods like `ToEntity`, `ToResponseDto`.
- Register mappers as Singleton; inject into handlers.

## Cancellation Tokens

- Pass `CancellationToken` through all async methods (handlers → repositories → infrastructure).
- Prefer `CancellationToken cancellationToken = default` on repository interfaces.

## Repositories

- Interfaces in Core (`Interfaces` or `Repositories`).
- Implementations in Infrastructure; register as Scoped.
